The machine-readable source for these results is <code>archive-v1/audit.json</code> and the complete row-level outputs are in <code>archive-v1/special/</code>.

== Category:BITwiki Templates ==
The generated category view reported seven members. The exact seven-member list is preserved in <code>archive-v1/categories/members/Category%3ABITwiki Templates.json</code>.

Those seven members are Main-namespace template-system documentation pages, not the nine implemented Template namespace pages. See [[BITwiki:Templates and categories]].

== Category List ==
[[Category List]] is an authored Main-namespace V1 page, not a generated Special page and not a <code>Category:</code> page. Its current exact wikitext and full public revision history are preserved in the exhaustive archive.

Its historical value is architectural: it explicitly separates domain categories, entity-type categories, BITwiki system categories and epistemic categories. V2 preserves those dimensions while assigning them to more appropriate primitives. See [[BITwiki:V1 category migration]].

== Pre/post migration use ==
Do not optimize for making every warning count equal zero. Use the reports to prove that every change is understood.

<pre>
refresh V1 archive
→ record baseline
→ import/migrate reviewed V2 material
→ rerun maintenance reports
→ diff counts + exact affected pages
→ explain intentional changes
→ correct accidental regressions
</pre>

Examples:
* a decrease in wanted categories should correspond to intentionally created V2 Category pages or migrated category membership;
* a decrease in unused templates should be caused by a deliberate caller migration, not arbitrary transclusion;
* a new broken redirect is a regression unless explicitly justified;
* an uncategorized Book Matter subpage may be acceptable when its parent/transclusion structure makes an additional category redundant.

== Source of truth ==
The live wiki generates the current Special-page views. The archive captures a reproducible historical baseline. Neither replaces the other.

